    {
      "type": "tweet",
      "id": "2018768089610174907",
      "url": "https://x.com/Stefano47271344/status/2018768089610174907",
      "twitterUrl": "https://twitter.com/Stefano47271344/status/2018768089610174907",
      "text": "$IONQ has a $20 billion annual revenue opportunity from data security alone if we focus on the areas where ordinary encryption isn’t considered strong enough for the most sensitive information.\nAnd considering that in the US market we can model a high growth company to have a P/S ratio of 30, then 20*30 = 600.\nBasically, we can justify a market cap of $600 billion for $IONQ considering only their services related to quantum security and communication without even accounting the quantum computing division (which will be the one that will bring the most revenue in the future).  \n\n$IONQ wouldn’t be protecting everyday consumer data or mass market cloud traffic, that’s handled by software-based post quantum encryption from Microsoft, Google, Cloudflare, and others.\nIONQ market is smaller but far more valuable per customer and this is key to understand.\n\nThe company would serve governments and institutions that need the highest level of security, including generating truly secure encryption keys, protecting classified intelligence and defense networks, securing satellites and space communications (including future lunar bases that NASA wants to build there), safeguarding central banks infrastructure, protecting institutional crypto holdings, and preserving critical records that must remain secret for decades.\n\nCustomers will include the US Department of Defense, Intelligence Community, and Space Force, core NATO allies, European governments, Canada, Australia, New Zealand, South Korea, GCC states like Saudi Arabia, UAE, Kuwait, Jordan, India, Singapore, and a few other allied countries like Argentina, Chile, Philippines ecc\n\nOrdinary post-quantum encryption doesn’t cover these systems, because Governments need solutions they can completely trust and that can’t be easily compromised. That’s where $IONQ comes in.\n\nCompetition exists, but it’s very limited: big tech handles mass-market security but isn’t trusted for top-secret government systems so they're out, Toshiba is strong in Japan but not in Western classified environments, and national solutions, where they exist, are fragmented and too early stage compared to IONQ. \n\n$IONQ doesn’t need to win everywhere, it just needs to be the trusted provider for the small slice of systems where failure is not an option. \nIn that segment, $20 billion in annual security related revenue is an achievable estimate.",
